  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1049 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nIncreasing cloudiness is expected into the overnight period with\na decaying cluster of storms currently to our north. CAM guidance\ncontinues to show this activity essentially dissipating by the\ntime it reaches our area, which follows the latest radar trends.\nThere will be a mention of isolated PoPs mainly along the TN/KY\nthrough portions of the overnight hours, but expect for\nessentially all of the Mid-South to remain dry and much of this\nactivity to either dissipate or stay east of the area. Into\nThursday, a rinse and repeat forecast is expected across the Mid-\nSouth with an upper-level trough continuing to push to the east.\nWhile some isolated thunderstorms cannot be ruled out in the\nafternoon, expect for these to occur during peak heating and\nquickly diminish following sunset. As for our temperatures,\ntemperatures will continue to climb a few more degrees with highs\nreaching the mid to upper 90s for the entire area. With dew point\ntemperatures remaining in the mid 70s, locations along and west\nof the MS River can expect for heat indices to climb above\n110 degrees, with 105 degrees or higher elsewhere. As such, an\nExtreme Heat Warning and Heat Advisory will remain in effect for\nThursday.\n\nBy Friday, an upper-level ridge will begin to build over the\nsoutheast with temperatures rising into the upper 90s to low 100s\nfor much of the Mid-South. Unfortunately, this trend will likely\ncontinue into at least the early parts of next week, if not the\nmiddle of next week. Guidance is still spread on the strength and\ntiming of a weak trough by midweek, which would allow\ntemperatures to be knocked back a few degrees. However,\ndecreasing temperatures a few degrees still means highs in the\nmid to upper 90s, so the oppressive heat is expected to continue\nfor the foreseeable future. With increasing confidence in\ntemperatures into the weekend, the Extreme Heat Warning and Heat\nAdvisory have been extended through at least Saturday. Further\nextension beyond Saturday will likely be needed, for at least a\nHeat Advisory, into early next week. As far as records go, the\ncurrent record high temperatures for our climate sites are well\ninto the low to mid 100s through this period. As such, it appears\nthat no daily maximum temperature records will likely be broken.\nRegardless of this, caution should be exercised if you must be\noutside for prolonged periods through at least the middle of next\nweek. Take actions now to be prepared for the rising\ntemperatures.",
